<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="testing-woven-code" title="Testing woven code">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>The testkit ships no JUnit. Declare JUnit 5 yourself, at test scope —
            <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a>.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        A test method takes a <code>Weaving</code>, weaves a class in memory, and asserts on the
        bytes that come back. No build step, no agent.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="java"><![CDATA[
package fixture;

import de.splatgames.aether.weaver.api.diagnostic.Severity;
import de.splatgames.aether.weaver.testkit.WeaveResult;
import de.splatgames.aether.weaver.testkit.WeaverExtension;
import de.splatgames.aether.weaver.testkit.Weaves;
import de.splatgames.aether.weaver.testkit.Weaving;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;

import static de.splatgames.aether.weaver.testkit.WovenAssert.assertThatWoven;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

@ExtendWith(WeaverExtension.class)
@Weaves(Audit.class)
class AuditTest {

    @Test
    void greetRunsTheHandler(final Weaving weaving) {
        final WeaveResult result = weaving.weave(Target.class);

        assertThatWoven(result)
                .wasWoven()
                .reportsNothing(Severity.WARNING)
                .satisfiesEveryInvariant()
                .preservesUntargetedMethods("greet")
                .loadsAndRuns(AuditTest::stillGreets);
    }

    static void stillGreets(final Class<?> woven) throws Exception {
        final Object it = woven.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
        assertEquals("hello", woven.getMethod("greet").invoke(it));
    }
}
]]></code-block>

    <chapter title="Where the bytes come from" id="bytes">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    L["Each class's own loader"] --> O["Original class file"]
    W["The weave classes you name"] --> P["Weaver planned once per test"]
    O --> P
    P --> R["WeaveResult, bytes before and after"]
    R --> D["Throwaway loader, one per defining call"]
    D --> H["Reflection reaches the injected handler"]
    L --> J["Loaded class, never redefined"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Both class files are read as resources. A target the JVM already holds weaves like
            one merely on the classpath. <a href="pipeline.topic">The weaving pipeline</a> covers
            what happens in between.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Add the testkit and run it" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Add both dependencies. The BOM from <a href="install.topic">Install</a> carries
                the testkit version. Any JUnit 5 version is yours to pick; this is the one the
                testkit is tested against.</p>
            <code-block lang="xml"><![CDATA[
<dependency>
    <groupId>%group%</groupId>
    <artifactId>aether-weaver-testkit</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
    <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
    <version>%junit%</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Put the target and the weave in your test sources.
                <a href="first-weave.topic">Your first weave</a> has both classes. Its
                <code>Audit</code> prints a line you can watch for.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Run the tests.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash">
mvn test
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>The handler runs inside the woven copy. Its output arrives under the test that
                loaded it.</p>
            <code-block lang="console">
[INFO] Running fixture.AuditTest
woven
[INFO] Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0, Time elapsed: 0.179 s -- in fixture.AuditTest
            </code-block>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="What each call in the chain checks" id="assertions">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="wasWoven()">
                <p>
                    Nothing applied. Put it first: <code>satisfiesEveryInvariant</code>,
                    <code>preservesUntargetedMethods</code> and <code>loadsAndRuns</code> read the
                    woven bytes and pass silently on the original when nothing did.
                    <code>reportsNothing</code> never reads bytes, so it can run before this one —
                    the only way to see the <code>AW1020</code> listing below.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="reportsNothing(Severity.WARNING)">
                <p>
                    Something was reported at that severity or worse. The failure lists each
                    diagnostic, with a remedy line where one was set.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="satisfiesEveryInvariant()">
                <p>
                    The bytes fail the verifier or the JVM, the two passes disagree
                    (<a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a>), the class version changed, or a
                    method lost a debug attribute it had.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title='preservesUntargetedMethods("greet")'>
                <p>
                    A method you did not name lost or changed its body. The failure quotes the
                    first differing instruction, the two lengths if one sequence is a prefix of
                    the other, or nothing if the method vanished.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="loadsAndRuns(callback)">
                <p>
                    A throwaway loader refused the bytes, or your callback threw. Reflection there
                    sees the woven members, not the loaded ones.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="When nothing was woven" id="nothing">
        <code-block lang="console">
[fixture.Target] nothing was woven into fixture.Target, so every
assertion after this one would be about the original class
        </code-block>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The weave never matched">
                <p>
                    <code>wasWoven()</code> fails first, with the message above. Drop it, or call
                    <code>reportsNothing</code> before it, and <code>AW1020</code> lists every
                    method the target declares — copy a signature from that listing.
                    <a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a> has the rest, and
                    <a href="selectors.topic">Selectors</a> the grammar.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Your build wove the class already">
                <p>
                    The <code>weave-tests</code> goal already rewrote
                    <code>target/test-classes</code>, and the loader hands that copy back. The
                    same plan is skipped in silence; a different one is refused as
                    <code>AW2201</code>. Bind only <code>weave</code>, never
                    <code>weave-tests</code>, if your test sources are to stay unwoven
                    (<a href="build-time-weaving.topic">Build-time weaving</a>).
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Pinning the bytes" id="golden">
        <code-block lang="java"><![CDATA[
GoldenFiles.in(Path.of("src/test/resources/golden"))
        .verify("target-audited", weaving.weave(Target.class));
]]></code-block>

        <p>
            The first run writes <code>target-audited.class</code> and a <code>javap</code>
            rendering beside it. It fails anyway, so that nobody commits a fixture nobody read.
            Later runs diff against it. Accept a change with a property, which asserts nothing
            while it is set.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="bash">
mvn test -Dgolden.update=true
        </code-block>
    </chapter>

